  },
  "order_matters": {
    "statement": "Engines differ in the order of these steps, for example whether temperature is applied before or after truncation. vLLM applies temperature, then min_p, then top_k/top_p, then samples. Top-k keeps the same set at any temperature (dividing by T keeps the ranking); top-p and min-p do not, because they look at probability values.",
    "top_p_before_temperature": [
      " KV",
      " cache",
      " GPU",
      " block"
    ],
    "top_p_after_temperature": [
      " KV",
      " cache",
      " block"
    ],
    "sources": [
      "research/notes/03-engines.md §2.4 (Sampler docstring, vllm/v1/sample/sampler.py) and §2.19 (Model Runner V2 keeps the same order)",
      "research/notes/05-papers-architecture.md §2.F (library ordering note)"
    ]
  },
  "where_it_runs": "vLLM samples on the GPU right after the LM head; only the chosen token ids (int32, 4 bytes per request) are copied to the CPU, not the [batch, vocab] logits. Source: research/notes/03-engines.md §2.4.",
  "seed_scope": "A per-request seed fixes the random draws. In Model Runner V2 the noise for a request depends only on (seed, position, token id), not on batch size or batch position. It does not fix the logits themselves; see data/nondeterminism.json. Source: research/notes/03-engines.md §2.19.",
  "min_p_caveat": "min-p is presented as a definition adopted by libraries; its quality claims are contested (research/notes/05-papers-architecture.md §2.F).",
